AOP = Aspect-Oriented Programming
--> used to separate cross-cutting concerns and business logic.
Cross-cutting concern : A functionality that is needed in many different parts of an application.

AOP Architecture Diagram
Client
   |
   v
Proxy Object
   |
   +----> Aspect (Logging, Security, Transaction)
   |
   v
Target Object (Business Logic)

1. Client calls a method
2. Request goes to Proxy
3. Proxy checks if any Aspect should run
4. Advice executes (Before/After/Around)
5. Actual method executes
6. Advice executes again if required
7. Result returned to Client

Aspect in Spring AOP :
An Aspect is a class that contains a cross-cutting concern (common functionality) such as:
Logging
Security
Transaction Management
Exception Handling
Performance Monitoring
--> Instead of writing this common code in every service class, we put it inside an Aspect.
--> Annotations used is @Aspect

Join Point in Spring AOP :
A Join Point is a point in the execution of your application where an Aspect can be applied.
In Spring AOP, a Join Point is usually a method execution.

Application Methods :
addEmployee()       ← Join Point
updateEmployee()    ← Join Point
deleteEmployee()    ← Join Point
calculateSalary()   ← Join Point
           |
           v
Pointcut selects some Join Points
           |
           v
Advice executes there

Advice in Spring AOP : Advice is the actual code that Spring executes at a Join Point.

Types of Advice

1. Before Advice
Runs before the method.

2. After Advice
Runs after the method.

3. After Returning Advice
Runs only if the method completes successfully.

4. After Throwing Advice
Runs when an exception occurs.

5. Around Advice
Runs both before and after the method.

Types of AOP :

1. Compile-Time Weaving
AOP code is added during compilation.

Source Code
     ↓
Compiler + Aspect
     ↓
Compiled Class

Advantage :
Very fast execution
Powerful AOP capabilities

2. Load-Time Weaving (LTW)
AOP code is added when the class is loaded into memory.

Class File
    ↓
Class Loader
    ↓
Aspect Added
    ↓
Memory

The original class file is not modified; weaving happens while loading.
Advantage :
No need to modify source code
More flexible than compile-time weaving

3. Runtime Weaving (Proxy-Based AOP)
AOP code is added while the application is running using proxies.

Client
   ↓
Proxy
   ↓
Target Object

This is the approach used by Spring AOP.
Advantage :
Easy to use
No special compiler needed
Built into Spring

Quick Comparison :
| Type                 | When AOP is Applied          |
| -------------------- | ---------------------------- |
| Compile-Time Weaving | During compilation           |
| Load-Time Weaving    | When class is loaded         |
| Runtime Weaving      | While application is running |

JDK Dynamic Proxy :
A JDK Dynamic Proxy is a proxy object created by Java at runtime that sits 
between the client and the actual object.
It intercepts method calls and can perform extra work like:
--> Logging
--> Security checks
--> Transactions
